{"page_content": "8 \n \nCool flame extinction is controlled primarily by chemical kinetics.  As the droplet size decreases, the residence \ntime in the reaction zone of the gas-phase diffusion flame is reduced to a value corresponding to which the cool \nflame becomes statically unstable, and this leads to cool flame extinction.  Figure 4 shows the cool flame \nextinction diameter as function of ambient pressure for both free-floated and fiber-supported droplets for pure \nn-dodecane burning in air. As mentioned earlier, as the pressure is increased above approximately 2 atm, \nreignitions were commonly observed, eventually leading to complete combustion of the fuel droplet.  It is \ninteresting to note that the 40 micron fiber shows relatively little effect on the extinction diameter. \n \n \n \n \nFigure 4. Cool flame extinction diameters as a function of ambient pressure for n-dodecane droplets  \nburning in air. \n \nSince the cool flame extinction is an instability phenomenon where the dynamic balance between heat release \nand heat loss from the flame is lost at the lower turning point in the NTC region, one would expect addition of \nhelium to have a substantial influence on the process. Figure 5 illustrates the effect of helium dilution on the \nextinction diameter for n-dodecane. Increase helium dilution leads to earlier cool flame extinction giving a larger \nextinction diameter.", "metadata": {"source": "CFI Science Final Report.pdf", "page": 9}}